    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d982">

                <pb facs="1241" n="1229" xml:id="pg1229"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">860h.24/5–1845: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State </hi><anchor n="78"
                        xml:id="d982-f1"/>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">to the Acting Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">San Francisco</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-05-18">May 18,
                        1945</date>.<lb/>[Received May 18—10:50 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>12. I received Subasic<anchor n="79" xml:id="d982-f2"/> this afternoon at his
                    request. He had submitted in writing a memorandum<anchor n="80" xml:id="d982-f3"
                    /> on the Yugoslav desire to receive Lend-Lease aid. I informed him that we
                    could not discuss questions such as Lend-Lease until the Venezia Giulia and
                    Carinthian situations had been cleared up. I emphasized the seriousness of the
                    situation and pointed out that at a time when we were trying to create an
                    organization for the peaceful and orderly settlement of disputes we could not
                    understand Yugoslav actions in this matter. I made clear, however, that the
                    eventual disposition of the territory in question was not involved in the
                    present issue.</p>
                <p>The Minister appeared to be very much disturbed but said that he had great
                    difficulty in maintaining communications with his Government. I said that in any
                    event the matter could not be discussed here and expressed the hope that a
                    prompt and favorable reply would be received to the note<anchor n="81"
                        xml:id="d982-f4"/> we had sent to his Government.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>[<hi rendition="#small-caps">Stettinius</hi>]</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d982-f1" n="78" type="source" xml:id="d982-f1-content">
                <p>The Secretary of State was attending the United Nations Conference on
                    International Organization, meeting at San Francisco, California, April 25–June
                    26, 1945. For documentation on this Conference, see <ref
                        target="frus1945v01#pg_1">vol. <hi rendition="#small-caps">i</hi>, pp.
                        1</ref> ff.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d982-f2" n="79" xml:id="d982-f2-content">
                <p>Ivan Subasich, the Yugoslav Foreign Minister, was a delegate to the San Francisco
                    Conference.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d982-f3" n="80" xml:id="d982-f3-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d982-f4" n="81" xml:id="d982-f4-content">
                <p>See telegram 86, May 14, 11 a.m., to Belgrade, <ref target="frus1945v04#pg_1161"
                        >vol. <hi rendition="#small-caps">iv</hi>, p. 1161</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
